And then from there, in the second half of this lecture, we'll cover the course logistics, and that part you should pay a lot of attention to because that influences your grades.

35
00:02:23,438 --> 00:02:27,620
So let's take a look at an overview of the trends for large language models.

36
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:40,240
So, one of the aspects that has been mind-blowing since GP3 came out is that as you scale up the number of parameters in a model, they get better.

37
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:50,148
And what this means is that language models like BERT and T5 were good, but as we increase the number of parameters, they got really better, they they performed much better.

38
00:02:50,414 --> 00:02:59,012
And what this led to is something called a scaling loss for large language models, which are used in building the base model for the pre-trained base model.

39
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:16,292
And as you increase the amount of compute, so there are three graphs here, as you increase the amount of compute on the x-axis, the loss function on the y-axis goes down, and the more compute you have put in, the more the test loss goes down, which leads to a better model.

40
00:03:16,590 --> 00:03:24,880
So that was one big aspect that led to GP3 and then all the subsequent Chat GPT, Palm, Gemini, and so on.

41
00:03:25,102 --> 00:03:32,190
And then similarly, as you increase the dataset size on X axis, the test loss goes down further on Y axis.

42
00:03:32,480 --> 00:03:38,084
So that's another aspect of like another axis of scaling that leads to better language models.

43
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:42,080
And uh the fourth one is of course, the third one is of course parameter count.

44
00:03:42,320 --> 00:03:52,240
So if you increase the number of layers in a transformer or the amount of parameters that are there in the language model, that improves the loss uh value and that leads to a better model.

45
00:03:52,334 --> 00:04:02,436
And this has been the foundation for a long time until last year, where this was starting to hit some kind of a saturation point.

46
00:04:04,206 --> 00:04:19,055
So from 2018 to almost 2024, this graph is a little bit outdated, but from 2018 to 2024, the as the model size has gone up almost consistently, we have been increasing the size of the model.

47
00:04:19,296 --> 00:04:38,736
So you started with something like BERT was 340 million parameters, GBD2 was 1.5 billion parameters, and then GBD3 was 175 billion parameters, and then say Palm was 540 billion, and then there was a bunch of subsequent models, and GBD4 is estimated to be there, which is like trillions of parameters.

48
00:04:38,896 --> 00:04:44,576
So you can imagine that there has been an exponential growth in just the model size for large language models.

49
00:04:44,910 --> 00:04:51,940
The large stands for that they're growing in size, and that has been one of the key areas in which the models have been getting better.

50
00:04:53,518 --> 00:04:56,656
So why do we care about the models getting bigger?

51
00:04:56,816 --> 00:04:57,936
What does this lead to?

52
00:04:58,096 --> 00:05:08,548
So, as I was explaining, that as the models get bigger, they continuously improve in performance on national language benchmarks, on various other benchmarks, reasoning benchmarks, and so on.

53
00:05:09,038 --> 00:05:15,460
And then the other interesting aspect that I will show you in a moment is that they learn how to do few-shot learning.

54
00:05:15,790 --> 00:05:29,056
So earlier you have to fine-tune the model for a specific domain, but just by giving a few examples, the model can follow that template and reason in that same vein, which makes it extremely easy to prototype things.

55
00:05:29,216 --> 00:05:37,732
And then the third aspect is that as the models become bigger, they have emerging behavior where the capabilities like reasoning only emerged in larger models.

56
00:05:39,822 --> 00:05:43,172
So just to understand few short learning or zero shot learning here.

57
00:05:43,616 --> 00:05:58,336
So if the model is given a question or a task like translate English to French, it's able to give so this is called a prompt, like it's given a task, translate English to French, and then it's asked to translate cheese to whatever is the French word.

58
00:05:58,496 --> 00:06:05,376
If the model can predict the answer without actually being trained on the specific task, then that would be called zero short learning.

59
00:06:05,550 --> 00:06:12,096
And then few short learning, in addition to giving the description of the task, you're also giving a few examples.

60
00:06:12,416 --> 00:06:22,256
in this particular case, it's given translate English to French and it's given a few examples of the translation from English to French, and then it's asked to translate cheese and it's able to translate that.

61
00:06:22,702 --> 00:06:38,500
And you can see that this capability shows up in the large language models across a large number of tasks, and that has been one of the key areas that GPT-3 and PAM and other models enabled, and then that subsequent innovations came from reasoning aspects of this.

62
00:06:39,502 --> 00:06:47,492
So, on the reasoning side, as models have become bigger, they have this emergent behavior of reasoning, which Azalia will tell us a little bit more about.

63
00:06:48,878 --> 00:06:50,656
So, as the models become bigger.

64
00:06:50,736 --> 00:07:08,896
not only we have this kind of a predictive scaling loss property that we kind of know how the loss function is gonna go down as we throw in more compute data and parameters in the model, there are new kind of behaviors that appear in the model that they didn't have before.

65
00:07:08,976 --> 00:07:14,576
and we never could predict that until we had these bigger models and saw these kind of behavior in them.

66
00:07:14,896 --> 00:07:23,524
And one of the most important one of those behaviors is the emergent is the chain of thought behavior.

67
00:07:23,776 --> 00:07:28,356
And for those of you who are not familiar with this, here is how it works.

68
00:07:28,656 --> 00:07:39,136
So, in a usual prompt prompting of a model, we can ask a model, in this case, a math problem, and then we can give it a one shot example.

69
00:07:39,296 --> 00:07:41,376
Here is a math problem, here is the answer.

70
00:07:41,646 --> 00:07:49,732
Now I ask you a similar but not exact, not exactly the same math problem, and the model is expected to answer that.

71
00:07:51,246 --> 00:07:55,536
and the model might be able to use the one shot example or it might not be.

72
00:07:55,790 --> 00:08:06,576
But we can do better here by not only give an example but also provide the reasoning or the chain of thought on how we got to that answer.

73
00:08:06,830 --> 00:08:10,416
So in this case, the question is a very simple question.

74
00:08:10,576 --> 00:08:18,336
Roger has five tennis balls, he buys two more cans of tennis balls, each can, each can has three tennis balls.

75
00:08:18,416 --> 00:08:21,616
How many, how many total tennis balls does he have now?

76
00:08:21,902 --> 00:08:26,148
And instead of just saying 11, like you're walking the model through how to get to this.

77
00:08:26,896 --> 00:08:33,856
Roger started with five balls, two cans of three tennis balls is six, and five plus six is eleven.

78
00:08:34,638 --> 00:08:45,604
and with this example, then the model has become familiar with the process to get to the answer, and it can leverage that to answer new problems.

79
00:08:46,416 --> 00:08:48,816
Well, obviously, this is a very simple problem.

80
00:08:48,976 --> 00:08:52,324
Any like 1B parameter model nowadays can solve this.

81
00:08:52,576 --> 00:08:56,736
Doesn't need an example or a few shot or a chain of thought.

82
00:08:56,896 --> 00:09:01,696
But the chain of thought property itself is a property that is holding to this day.

83
00:09:01,902 --> 00:09:13,412
And it's a very, very important property for the reasoning models and all the thinking models and all the prog and many much of the progress that we have seen in the past year or so.

84
00:09:14,798 --> 00:09:20,416
Now this property again appears as we have larger and larger models.

85
00:09:20,576 --> 00:09:23,908
Here are three models: lambda, GPT, and Palm.

86
00:09:24,174 --> 00:09:30,096
And as you can see, the chain of thought, and this is some results on the math, some math data set.

87
00:09:30,496 --> 00:09:44,388
And these models, when they're smaller, like 8 billion parameter for lambda or for GPT around 7 billion, they can't really benefit from chain of thought, like it doesn't do anything for them.

88
00:09:44,736 --> 00:10:01,572
But as they become larger, or for a palm mother again at 8 billion, it can leverage this chain of thought property and learn from the reasoning and the process that is provided in the context in order to solve problems better.

89
00:10:03,376 --> 00:10:10,016
And not only that, there are other kinds of abilities to solve new tasks as we increase the size of the models.

90
00:10:10,176 --> 00:10:23,056
Like, for example, models can all of a sudden solve mod arithmetics better or solve a word unscrambled problem just by having this larger size and this property.

91
00:10:23,216 --> 00:10:26,596
You see that all of a sudden it appears at a certain size.

92
00:10:27,296 --> 00:10:45,444
And because of all of this, we still, as in Frontier companies and labs, are still interested in pushing the scaling laws and not only get the natural kind of progression, but also we will see more and more of these kind of emergent behavior that we are.

93
00:10:45,696 --> 00:10:48,096
uh, that is going to be very important.

94
00:10:49,518 --> 00:10:52,096
Now, a little bit of history here.

95
