00:11:57,376 --> 00:12:09,456
so let's first and then um we're gonna learn about all of these in more detail, but let's just take a step back and see how the process works.

101
00:12:09,616 --> 00:12:16,932
So the pre-training step is the step with the beginning of any language model training.

102
00:12:17,536 --> 00:12:24,964
that's the easiest step, and we just train the model to predict the next token out of all sorts of text and data and so on.

103
00:12:25,518 --> 00:12:32,324
And then this is the fine-tuning step that was a key differentiating factor for a model like ChatGPT.

104
00:12:33,614 --> 00:12:42,724
and let's start with the first aspect of fine tuning, which is kind of alignment with human preferences.

105
00:12:43,502 --> 00:13:03,056
So when a model is pre-trained, it has no sense, it has seen all data on the internet, on the book, in the books, everywhere, but it has no sense of like what is right and wrong and what is, it just has it just statistically knows about the state of the world, but it doesn't exactly know things or know how to follow instructions.

106
00:13:03,376 --> 00:13:17,156
So the next step in training these models is the step where we try to steer the AI models to follow the goals and preferences and values of humans.

107
00:13:18,894 --> 00:13:21,776
and this is still a big problem, it's a big issue.

108
00:13:21,966 --> 00:13:34,692
We haven't mastered it yet, but as the models become, as we progress, models are becoming better and much more kind of like Powerful in the things that they can do.

109
00:13:35,376 --> 00:13:39,056
So it turns out that we can fine tune the models to be aligned.

110
00:13:39,136 --> 00:13:50,948
For example, here are some graphs that show what if we take our base model that's just pre-trained and fine-tune it for things like sensibleness or safety.

111
00:13:51,616 --> 00:14:05,536
And these are like these are data that we have collected, these are high-quality data for kind of showing the model what we mean by what's safe and what's unsafe, or what's sensible and what doesn't make sense.

112
00:14:05,742 --> 00:14:17,856
And these are like more curated, like more highly curated data that we pass to the model for fine-tuning and to make the model more aligned with the values or the or the preferences of humans.

113
00:14:21,296 --> 00:14:26,468
So going back, let's just walk through this process again.

114
00:14:27,056 --> 00:14:43,556
So the first stop when we take a pre-trained language model is fine tuning with next token prediction, which follows the same process as pre-training, but this time on data that is much more higher quality.

115
00:14:44,558 --> 00:14:57,136
For example, this could be data from books, creative essays, data that companies pay a whole lot, millions of dollars or hundreds of millions of dollars, potentially to buy this data.

116
00:14:57,136 --> 00:14:58,496
that's really high quality.

117
00:14:58,702 --> 00:15:04,900
and then we train the we fine-tune the model on those, and the model becomes much better as a result of that.

118
00:15:08,270 --> 00:15:11,076
the next step is instruction tuning.

119
00:15:11,438 --> 00:15:27,908
And by instruction tuning, what we mean is that we show the model again traditionally in the past two years, this instruction tuning data is like a combination of human-generated data or templates and the synthetic data.

120
00:15:28,110 --> 00:15:41,856
But the way this instruction fine-tuning works is that we do have instruction and then question-answer pairs where the model learns how to follow the questions and answers them.

121
00:15:42,016 --> 00:15:47,856
For example, here an instruction tuning data set could be about the weather.

122
00:15:47,936 --> 00:15:54,496
Like, please answer the following question: what is the boiling point of nitrogen, and then this is the answer.

123
00:15:54,976 --> 00:16:11,204
or we can have chain of thought fine tuning where we show the model how it can walk through a process to get to the answer, and then we have the label, we have the results over here, and this is like these pairs of like questions and instructions and answers.

124
00:16:11,616 --> 00:16:27,536
And again, this this data set is very um there's a lot of effort that goes into this data set, and the quality of the data set and the generality of it has a lot of impact on the quality of the model that we get at this stage.

125
00:16:28,718 --> 00:16:35,456
and after this point, then the model becomes more and more like um like the models we see today.

126
00:16:35,616 --> 00:16:39,536
Like you can ask them questions, you can go back and forth with them.

127
00:16:39,696 --> 00:16:49,732
They have this sense of like how to perceive a question and how to walk through kind of uh some sort of like process to get to an answer.

128
00:16:50,976 --> 00:17:01,616
And the next step after instruction fine-tuning is this process of using human preferences and fine-tuning the model based on that.

129
00:17:01,838 --> 00:17:07,136
And this process is called RLHF or reinforcement learning from human feedback.

130
00:17:08,013 --> 00:17:14,896
the way this is different from the previous system is the way we create the objective function and create the data.

131
00:17:15,136 --> 00:17:26,692
So in this case, instead of like just creating some supervised prompt and labels, we are basically creating a reward model out of human preferences.

132
00:17:27,136 --> 00:17:41,348
So companies spend a lot of money asking humans, sometimes experts, sometimes just Humans, normal humans, about questions and the answers generated by the model.

133
00:17:41,710 --> 00:17:47,376
And we ask the humans to rate them to see which one is correct and which one is not correct.

134
00:17:47,536 --> 00:17:51,428
And that's how we can create some sort of a reward model here.

135
00:17:52,398 --> 00:18:06,096
And this reward model is instead, instead of human, then we can use the reward model in order to kind of like guide the parameters of our LLM towards generating answers that are in line with this reward model.

136
00:18:06,176 --> 00:18:13,956
So basically, we want the generations by the model to be such that this reward model says, yes, these are good generations.

137
00:18:14,656 --> 00:18:22,628
We're gonna learn more about this here, but we wanted you to have the high-level idea of what RLHF means.

138
00:18:23,056 --> 00:18:27,456
And we can collect, we can have different reward types, different types of reward.

139
00:18:27,776 --> 00:18:38,656
For example, we could ask the model to reward a pair of answers based on their correctness or helpfulness or specificity or harmlessness, all sort of reward functions that we can define.

140
00:18:38,830 --> 00:18:49,188
And depending on how we want the models to be and what we care most about, we can weight these different reward models and kind of use that in the RLHF process.

141
00:18:50,256 --> 00:18:55,776
So these steps at a high level, of course, there's a lot of effort goes into that.

142
00:18:55,886 --> 00:19:18,440
but like pre-training and then fine-tuning on higher quality data, instruction, tuning and RLHF are the core kind of components that made a model like ChatGPT over something that existed over all the large models, like GPT-3 and prior models.

143
00:19:18,576 --> 00:19:22,820
So that made a huge difference in how good and capable the models were.

144
00:19:26,478 --> 00:19:31,936
So that was very cool up until last year or a year, a year, year and a half ago.

145
00:19:32,206 --> 00:19:34,820
Pre-training and then fine-tuning were the big pieces.

146
00:19:35,776 --> 00:19:43,652
but since a year and a half ago, it turns out inference is also a frontier for making the models much more capable.

147
00:19:46,990 --> 00:19:54,416
and that opened a whole lot of new research and directions and ways that we can make the model better.

148
00:19:55,406 --> 00:19:58,896
so let's talk about it a little bit.

149
00:19:59,056 --> 00:20:04,576
Like this is the work we did in my lab last year, last summer.

150
00:20:04,816 --> 00:20:07,296
It was called Large Language Monkeys.

151
00:20:08,016 --> 00:20:13,776
and it's kind of inspired by the infinite monkey theorem, and it's related to inference scaling.

152
00:20:14,766 --> 00:20:17,604
how many of you have heard of the infinite monkey theorem?

153
00:20:18,896 --> 00:20:20,256
Okay, that's a good amount.

154
00:20:20,416 --> 00:20:26,336
So the idea of the infinite monkey theorem, what it says is that it's not proven.

155
00:20:26,496 --> 00:20:42,496
but what it says is that if you have a monkey and a kind of a typing machine, and then we keep letting the monkey type all day, forever, after some time, we will have the works of William Shakespeare in the things that the monkey types.

156
00:20:43,470 --> 00:21:00,452
so we were inspired by that to name our project, but the idea here was that let's have the LLM be the monkey and let's ask the model over and over again to solve a given input problem.

157
00:21:01,486 --> 00:21:04,896
you can think of it as parallel sampling or parallel generation.

158
00:21:05,070 --> 00:21:09,860
So instead of asking the model once to solve the input problem, you can ask it many times.

159
00:21:10,158 --> 00:21:20,896
And also let's assume you have some sort of a verifier or selection mechanism so you can pick which one of these generated responses were correct.

160
00:21:21,456 --> 00:21:23,696
and then you output that in your system.

161
00:21:23,856 --> 00:21:26,084
That becomes the final output.

162
00:21:26,176 --> 00:21:36,176
So the model, instead of generating one answer, generates many, many answers, and then your verifier selects which one of them is correct and then outputs one of the correct ones.

163
00:21:36,576 --> 00:21:39,748
you can think of a verifier as, say, some unit test.

164
00:21:40,046 --> 00:21:51,300
If the problem is generating code, the verifier could be the unit test that we run against the code and we see which one of the code generations passes all the unit tests and so on.

165
00:21:53,230 --> 00:22:07,056
the reason this is very, this is a it this can be a possible possibility here is that models are uh do not are not uh there is some variance in the way the models generate responses.

166
00:22:07,278 --> 00:22:08,388
It's not deterministic.

167
00:22:08,654 --> 00:22:13,668
And you can even control the variation in the model responses with this thing called temperature.

168
00:22:13,966 --> 00:22:21,444
So you can force the model or encourage the model to generate more different responses as you ask a question.

169
00:22:23,918 --> 00:22:28,804
And here now let's see some of the results of this repeated sampling.

170
00:22:29,198 --> 00:22:42,672
For a bunch of tasks like math and coding benchmarks, we increase the number of samples per problem from one, which is the normal one, to 10,000.

171
00:22:43,150 --> 00:22:50,672
And here we are showing the coverage or the fraction of problems that are solved by at least one of these samples.

172
00:22:50,912 --> 00:22:57,632
And what we are seeing here is that so the red line, the red dashed one was the GPT-4.

173
00:22:59,552 --> 00:23:10,592
And while these blue and green lines, which were the LAMA 8B, Lama 38B, and 7EB, were worse than GPT 4.0 model with one sample.

174
00:23:10,672 --> 00:23:20,032
but if we increase the number of samples from these models, in all of these cases, they do better than the GPT.

175
00:23:20,112 --> 00:23:21,156
4.0 model.

176
00:23:21,952 --> 00:23:32,992
And what it says is that it kind of seems like the models already know a whole lot more than what you get out of them when you just ask them once, right?

